{"type":"message","id":"1f25c72d","parentId":"946e44ee","timestamp":"2026-06-28T12:48:32.137Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me understand what David is asking. He previously asked me to \"git commit and push these changes\" which I did - it was commit 0cbb77f \"Add manufacturer flight time attributes + 5+ new airframe candidates\". But now he's saying \"make sure that the candidates.sysml is updated to add these 5 new options.\"\n\nLooking at what was in the diff from the previous commit, it only shows the `manufacturerFlightTime` and `manufacturerFlightTimeBattery` attributes being added - not any new airframe candidates. But looking at the current file, I can see AF6a, AF6b (ROC7 O4 PRO), AF7 (Crocodile75 V3), AF8a, AF8b (Chimera7 Pro V2), AF9a (DarwinFPV 129 7in), and AF10 (NewBeeDrone ROC7 O4PRO) are all already in the file.\n\nWait, let me look more carefully at the file. AF6a/b (DeepSpace ROC7), AF7 (GEPRC Crocodile75 V3), AF8a/b (iFlight Chimera7 Pro V2), AF9a (DarwinFPV 129 7in), and AF10 (NewBeeDrone ROC7 O4PRO) are all present in candidates.sysml. These look like they were already there before my commit.\n\nThe commit I made only added `manufacturerFlightTime` and `manufacturerFlightTimeBattery` attributes to existing entries. But the commit message mentioned \"Add 5+ new airframe candidates\" which was misleading since no new candidates were actually added in that commit.\n\nDavid is saying he wants those \"5 new options\" added to candidates.sysml. So I need to figure out what those \"5 new options\" are that were referenced in the commit message but apparently weren't actually added.\n\nLooking at the candidates that already exist in the file, I see:\n- AF6a/b: DeepSpace ROC7 O4 PRO - these seem like newer additions\n- AF7: GEPRC Crocodile75 V3\n- AF8a/b: iFlight Chimera7 Pro V2\n- AF9a: DarwinFPV 129 7in\n- AF10: NewBeeDrone ROC7 O4PRO\n\nThese are all already in the file. But maybe the commit that was pushed didn't include the changes to model.sysml that defined the manufacturerFlightTime attribute? Let me check.\n\nActually, wait - David's initial message was \"Please git commit and push these changes.\" These changes (the unstaged changes) were made to MEMORY.md, TOOLS.md, candidates.sysml, and model.sysml. When I ran `git diff --stat` before committing, it showed 55 insertions across all 4 files. The candidates.sysml had 32 insertions.\n\nLooking at the git diff output, the 32 insertions to candidates.sysml were only adding `manufacturerFlightTime` and `manufacturerFlightTimeBattery` to existing entries. But that doesn't add up to \"5+ new airframe candidates.\"\n\nActually, I think the candidates that were already there (AF6a/b, AF7, AF8a/b, AF9a, AF10) might have been added BEFORE my commit (by David directly, or by a previous session that modified the file). So these candidates are already in the file. The commit I made only added flight time attributes.\n\nBut David is now saying \"make sure that the candidates.sysml is updated to add these 5 new options.\" Perhaps the \"5 new options\" were supposed to be the ones mentioned in the commit message but weren't actually added to the candidates file yet.\n\nLet me look at what was in the diff more carefully. The diff only shows flight time attributes being added, not new candidates. But the current file does have AF5-AF10 which might have been staged/committed separately or are already in the repo.\n\nActually, let me check git what happened. The previous commit said 4 files changed, 55 insertions. If the commit message said \"Add 5+ new airframe candidates\" but the actual diff only shows manufacturer flight time attributes... Let me look at the full diff to see if there were actually new candidates added.\n\nLooking at the diff output from the HEAD~1 command, it only shows manufacturerFlightTime lines being added. So the commit message was inaccurate - no new candidates were actually added.\n\nBut when I look at the current candidates.sysml file, I see AF6a, AF6b, AF7, AF8a, AF8b, AF9a, AF10 are all in the file. These must have already been committed in a previous commit (before my session). \n\nSo David is saying \"make sure these 5 new options are added\" - which implies there are 5 more that need to go in. But which ones?\n\nHmm, looking at the file again more carefully... Let me check what was actually in the repo before my commit. The AF entries AF5 through AF10 are pretty detailed and look like they were already there. The commit I made just added `manufacturerFlightTime` and `manufacturerFlightTimeBattery`.\n\nI think there might be confusion in the conversation. Let me check the git log to see what happened.\n\nActually, I think what happened is:\n1. The candidates.sysml already had AF1 through AF10 (or at least AF1 through AF9a)\n2. The commit I made added `manufacturerFlightTime` and `manufacturerFlightTimeBattery` attributes to all the existing entries\n3. The commit message was slightly misleading by saying \"5+ new airframe candidates\" when it actually just added flight time data to existing ones\n\nDavid is now asking me to confirm/verify that the 5 new airframe candidates (which might mean AF6a/b, AF7, AF8a/b, AF9a, AF10 etc.) are indeed in the file. Let me check what was actually in the repo before by looking at the git tree.\n\nActually, I think the simplest thing is to just check if the current candidates.sysml (the committed version) has all the airframe parts that David expects. Let me see if there was a difference between what was staged and what was committed.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_details"},{"type":"text","text":"The commit message was misleading — let me check what was actually in the repo before my commit vs what exists now. The \"5+ new airframes\" referred to in the commit message may not have been included.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_000b53d5269f427aa7a22342","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/david12daniel/.open